 Okay, now let's have a look at these other concerns, the non-euro, the non-financial.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 So here we see some examples of these non-monetary concerns.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 For instance, for the public sector, why is a governmental entity deciding to build a bridge or a tunnel?

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 Not to make profit, but they do it to speed up traffic flows, to reduce congestion, to improve safety.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 They invest in water resources, to have more drinking water, to meet irrigation needs, to improve wastewater treatment,

6
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 to make parts of the city more interesting for industrial development.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 So these are the public sector examples.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 Now you see the examples of the private sector. We will cover some of them.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 So what are the issues there? The issues are there like environmental concern, occupational safety, automation, innovation, image, competitiveness.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 Okay, so let me draw your attention to this.

11
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 If you look in the book, in your handbook, you will see not a very extensive coverage of what we are seeing here in the remainder of this chapter.

12
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 But of course, for the exam, I expect you to know what we've been seeing in class.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 I do not expect you to limit yourself to what is in the book.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 Okay, so cost effectiveness. What is effectiveness? Costs we already know. What are effectiveness?

15
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 Well, effectiveness has to do with some results, with outputs of a project that we are doing.

16
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 So these benefits are non-monetary, but in this case, they're still quantitative.

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 For instance, percentage of risk reduction, percentage of throughput increase, response time, amount of pollution removed, number of occupational accidents avoided.

18
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 Of course, they need to be relevant and we need to have them quantitative and objective and also practical.

19
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 Maybe you can come up with a very nice quantitative measure, but if we can't measure it or it would require a lot of extra work to measure that, we will not use it because it's impractical.

20
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 So the basic idea, and this is a conceptual figure here, is we have effectiveness and we have costs and we have different projects that are the dots.

21
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 And how do we use the cost effectiveness analysis? Well, we can use it in two ways.

22
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 Either we have a threshold, E, and we are saying, well, we are only interested in projects that are above a certain effectiveness level.

23
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 They could say, well, I'm only interested in safety improving projects if they improve safety by more than 10% or if they can at least have the number of occupational accidents.

24
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 So we can look at it in that way. Or we could say we have different projects, but we have a budget limitation.

25
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 So we can only consider projects that are total cost us more than that amount.

26
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 So that's the idea. So for a given budget, we want to maximize the effectiveness or for a given effectiveness level that we want to reach, we want to minimize the cost.

27
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 Also in healthcare, by the way, this is a cost effectiveness is used.

28
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 Costs or costs outcomes here are in natural units like, for instance, live years gained or points blood pressure or the level of blood glucose.

29
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:29,000
 And here also we have a cost effectiveness ratio, which is the cost of an intervention over the effectiveness of the intervention and an incremental cost effectiveness ratio,

30
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:38,000
 which is the incremental one, in fact, so B minus A for cost and B minus A for effectiveness.

31
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 If you remember, we saw the tornado diagram in the chapter on risk showing this incremental cost effective ratio.

32
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 Cost effectiveness plain is also popular there in healthcare.

33
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 In healthcare, I mean, suppose that we have a treatment, a current treatment for a disease.

34
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
 And then we have some that's our defender and we have some challenges.

35
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 So a new treatment, then we are looking where the new treatment is situated and we have an effectiveness axis and we have a cost axis.

36
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 If the new treatment is here, it means that it's costing more and is less effective than the old one.

37
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
 So we definitely will not go for it.

38
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:38,000
 If it's here, we will do it because it's more effective and it's costing less.

39
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 If it's in this race, in this area, we will have to see it's more effective, more costly or less costly and less effective.

40
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:55,000
 We will have to see what is the more important one, the difference in cost or the difference in effectiveness.

41
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 OK, cost effectiveness analysis for multiple projects.

42
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 We can make a difference between independent projects or mutually exclusive alternatives.

43
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 Well, let's have a look.

44
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 This is procedure for independent alternatives.

45
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 As usual, let's go to an example that's more clear than explaining this text.

46
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 This is an example for independent projects.

47
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 The case study is about Rollings Foundation for Innovative Thinking.

48
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 They allocate one million in grants to award companies that enroll their R&D personnel in a professional training program.

49
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 As they are keen on rewarding top R&D, they want to use the number of patents approved each year by the companies as an effectiveness measure.

50
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
 Six companies asked for grants and submitted a program proposal.

51
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 So in this table, the proposals are numbered from one to six.

52
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 For each proposal, the proposed number of attendees is given as well the cost per person.

53
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 And the last column provides a historical track record of the program graduates.

54
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 As mentioned, this is what the Rollings Foundation is very interested in.

55
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
 OK, let's have a look at the solution.

56
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:38,000
 So what happened here in the table is that the cost effectiveness ratio per patent was computed.

57
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
 Of course, not so difficult.

58
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 We have the costs and we have the patents per graduate, so we can compute the cost effectiveness ratio.

59
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 And then a ranking was made.

60
00:07:49,000 --> 00:08:02,000
 Then the total cost of the program was computed, which is simple, which is the cost per person times the number of people that intend to be included in the program.

61
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
 And we can start.

62
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
 So we have 60,000 for the first program.

63
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 We have 217,500 if we also include the second program.

64
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
 If you want to include the third program, which here is program five.

65
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
 Actually, we have 283.

66
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
 We still have some space, so we can include the next one.

67
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 And then we have 739.

68
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
 We still have some money left.

69
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:41,000
 But then if you want to include the full program six, we see that we have a problem because we only have one million available.

70
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,000
 So we will not be able to completely include that sixth one.

71
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 So OK, so that's how it works.

72
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
 Not very complicated.

73
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 Mutual exclusive alternatives.

74
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
 Let's have a look there as well.

75
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 Again, you have the procedure.

76
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:08,000
 I think that the example will make a lot clear.

77
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:22,000
 The example is about one of the corporations, which was not selected for external funding, and they decided to fund 50 of their R&D people to attend the innovation and creativity programs at their own expense.

78
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 Important criterion is that the program must have a historical average for a graduate of at least two patterns per year.

79
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:37,000
 And we are asked to use the data in the table to select the best program.

80
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 So these are the programs with more than two patterns a year, cost per person.

81
00:09:47,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 The 50 people that they attend and then we can compute the cost effectiveness ratio, which is three column three divided by column four and six is a program cost, which is two times three, as we have seen in the previous example.

82
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
 Now, what is the procedure?

83
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:13,000
 Well, we will rank the alternatives by increasing effectiveness, which is the patterns number of patterns per year.

84
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:23,000
 And then we compare the cost effectiveness ratio for program four, which is a challenger to do nothing, the defender.

85
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 But we see that the program cost per person divided by the patterns per graduate is 1190.

86
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
 So it seems interesting.

87
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
 So let's do program four.

88
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:46,000
 Now program four becomes the defender program five, the challenger.

89
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:56,000
 If you do incremental analysis, we come to the conclusion that we have again a positive number.

90
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 So it means that five is more expensive, but we also get more patterns.

91
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 So it's not very clear.

92
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 There's no clear dominance.

93
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
 So we keep for the time being, we keep both programs.

94
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
 Then we compare five to program two.

95
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:19,000
 We see that we have a negative cost effectiveness.

96
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 So that means that we have a cheaper increment.

97
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 We have more patterns and we spend less money.

98
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
 So we will eliminate program five.

99
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
 Then we have two and four lifts.

100
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
 So we compare two and four.

101
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
 Again, we have a positive number.

102
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:48,000
 So there's no dominance, which means that in this case, our cost effectiveness analysis does not provide us with a clear answer.

103
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:54,000
 So we will have to look further, maybe include some other non-financial criteria.

